# Design docs

## Locations

| Document | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `docs/requirements.ja.md` | Canonical requirements (Japanese). What the library must do and how well |
| `docs/design.ja.md` | Canonical design (Japanese). Contracts, invariants, phases |
| `docs/design-decisions.md` | Rejected alternatives and why (Decision / Reason / Rejected) |
| `docs/adr/NNNN-*.md` | One decision per file whose reason is a measurement (Status, Date, Issue, Context, Decision) |
| `docs/roadmap.md` | Rows: ID, title, Issue, status. No acceptance text |
| `docs/references.md` | Which upstream file or paper to read before a layer is built |
| `docs/conventions.md` | Directory tree, project responsibilities, coding conventions |

`.dev/` is local scratch and is gitignored. Do not put lasting decisions there.

## Changing the design

- Requirement, contract, public API, invariant, or phase: open a `design` Issue, update `docs/requirements.ja.md` or `docs/design.ja.md` (and `docs/design-decisions.md` for what was rejected) in its PR, and merge it before implementation Issues.
- Wording, typos, and examples: may ship in the same PR as code.
- Do not implement first and update the design later. If code and design conflict, stop and fix the design through the process above.
- Before an implementation PR is called ready, read each section of the design that its Issue names against the code, item by item: every signature and constraint in a code block, every row of a table, every listed input or kind. An item the code does differently gets the design fixed (wording) or the code fixed in the same PR; an item the Issue's scope leaves out goes to the owner as a question. In C1 a review found three: a generic constraint the code block lacked, a mapping unit the storage table stated otherwise, and a pointer that the zero-copy list named and no Issue built.
- That reading includes the statement behind every test whose expected value the PR changes. PR #181 raised the bound on the labels a band axis measures from `Count + 2` to `3 · Count + 3`, and design §4.6 and the G7b row of §10 still said two per k cut short.
- A design PR writes what the Grill decided. A better shape found while writing does not replace a decision in the PR, even with a note in its body: it goes to the owner as a question with a recommendation (`.cursor/rules/consent.mdc`), before the PR is opened, and the owner's answer goes into the Issue's Decision in the same push as the design. PR #205 opened with three such changes listed under "points to confirm" in its body (a stacked layer's baseline, the dodge of a numeric x's error bars, the corrected Decision 13), and the review made it a Should fix.
- A design that lets a set of options apply to a set of targets (positions to geoms, scales to channels) writes each pair that is refused, not only the targets that take none. PR #205 refused positions for the geoms that take none and left out area's `Dodge` and the error bar's `Stack`, and the review found the gap. The channels a layer has are targets too: PR #215 gave stats to geoms and left out a stat layer's numeric `ColorBy`, which has no row to color.
- A design PR that changes a decision searches the whole design for the sections that restate it, and changes them in the same PR: the diagrams, the tables of failures, the test sections, and the examples included. PR #137 moved the 4×4 public contract from G10 to R3 and left §13.1's G4 row saying "in G10".
- That search uses the shortest word that names the concept, and its synonyms, not a phrase from one sentence: a search for 「画面へのアフィン」 missed 「アフィン変換で求める」 in another section.
- Every line the search finds is either changed or named in the PR body with the reason it stays. PR #27's search found 「BenchmarkDotNet と End-to-end」 in `docs/conventions.md` and left it, and the review had to ask.
- A design that changes the shape of a type rewrites every example in the design that uses the type so that it works with the new shape; changing the comment beside it is not enough. A design whose text names a member of a type adds that member to the type in the same section's code block.
- A design that adds public types adds the row's line to the per-row list of design §4.9 and to the public surface of `.cursor/rules/csharp.mdc`, both in the same PR. PR #137 added them to `csharp.mdc` only; PR #169 added them to §4.9 only, and the review asked for `csharp.mdc`; PR #205 did the same again.
- A note that tells the reader to read a section with a substitution ("read W as H, a pixel column as a pixel row") is checked against each item of that section. PR #137's note fit the geoms along x and was the wrong way round for the horizontal rule, which a flip makes vertical (4W and pixel columns, not 4H and rows).
- A design that adds a property of a reference type to a spec record adds it to the null check of the spec (design §4.8). The builder's check does not see a spec made with `with { Coord = null! }`; PR #137 left it to the review.
- Before a row's design is written, the whole design is searched for the row's ID, and every contract an earlier row sent to it is taken into the new section.
  - A row split or renamed is searched the same way, and every mention of the old ID is pointed at the new row. PR #231 split G6 into G6a, G6b, and G6c, and §4.6's scope of row G3 and §4.9's `.FacetWrap` (G6) still named G6; the review found them.
- A rule applied to part of a family names the members it leaves out and why, above all those that share its mechanism, and says it in the form the code takes (not applied, rather than applied with no effect). PR #177 held the axes' end labels and did not name the color bar, whose ticks use the same `TickAxis`; it said the polar radius axis applied the rule with no effect, and the code passed it no room. An end of an axis that can be reversed is named so that it reads the same reversed: "the low end (pixel 0; right or top when reversed)", not "the start".
- A design that leaves a point to another row ("row X decides") names a row that exists in `docs/roadmap.md`, and that row's text names the point, in the same PR (`.cursor/rules/defer.mdc`).
- A new design checks each invariant of design §11 that it touches, one by one, and writes an exception into the invariant's own sentence. In C2, `Dispose` waited for a worker on the UI thread against invariant 7 until a review found it.
- A design PR that adds a sentence of contract adds the test that checks it to the row of design §10 in the same PR (`.cursor/rules/spec-checks.mdc`). The C2 design and its correction (#10, #21) both left it to a review. So does an implementation PR that adds a sentence of contract to the design: PR #142 wrote which axis each tick spacing takes under flip, and the check of `DrawColumns`'s `axis`, with tests and without the row's items, and the review asked for them.
- A design that readies a contract for a later row separates the contract's shape, which the current row builds, from the implementation behind it, which the later row builds. The current row implements only what one of its requirements asks; the later row's text names the rest. D1 first had v1's shaders take depth and 3-component positions that no v1 requirement draws.
- A sentence added to the design to answer a review is a claim like any other and is checked before it goes in.
- After a script edits a Markdown table, read `git diff --stat`. A diff of the whole table means its columns were padded again: restore the file and replace only the rows that change.

## Claims about other implementations

- A comparison of approaches in the design includes the approach of each library that a measurement found fastest, judged by the requirements, not only a reason of structure.
- A claim about what another implementation has or does (a library version, a chart type, an aggregation, a GPU path) names the file and line, or the documentation page or the paper's section, where it was read, and is read there before it goes in. PR #129 wrote that a zero-length run's square cap was "as in Canvas" without reading it; Canvas drops zero-length subpaths, and the axis-aligned square is SVG 2's (§9.4). The claim keeps the conditions the source states: "it does not redraw a frame that is not dirty" is wrong when the source redraws under a condition the sentence leaves out. PR #137 wrote "as ggplot2's `coord_flip`" with no page; the reference page of `coord_flip` is now quoted.
- A claim a Grill question makes (what another implementation does, as a reason for an option) is read in its source before the round is sent, as a claim in the design is. So is a parameter of an algorithm a question offers: PR #112's Q14 set the target of Talbot 2010's tick search as a count, and the paper, read only when the design was written, takes a density; the decision had to be asked again (Q16).
- A paper and its authors' code can differ. Talbot 2010's pseudocode takes a target density ρt and names its bounds without formulas; a review recalled a form that takes a count m and gives the bounds, which the paper does not have. A claim names the one that was read, and a form a review recalls from memory is checked in the text before the design changes.
- A rule, an algorithm, or a list taken from a specification or a paper (M4, an LTTB variant, a tick algorithm, a color space) is copied from its section, read for the purpose, and the section is named. Read the text itself (download the paper or the page and read the section), not a summarizing tool's answer, which drops parts and may describe an older version.
- A design does not say what all the compared libraries do or lack when one of them was not read: read it (fetch the file at the same commit) or leave it out of the claim.
- A sentence that says a list is the same as another implementation's is checked item by item in that implementation's source, and an item it lacks is written as HyperPlot's own. A design that says it does what another implementation does names the parameters that are the same.
- A default taken from another implementation is checked against the HyperPlot parts it works with, not only against that implementation. matplotlib's default image extent (−0.5, w − 0.5, h − 0.5, −0.5) has a meaning together with matplotlib's axes, which it turns upside down; HyperPlot's layers do not turn an axis, so the same default drew the image upside down. PR #197 found it after the Grill and asked the owner again.
- How a cross-platform library draws on one OS is read on that OS's path (the backend, the rasterizer it uses there), and a path behind a build or an OS condition is checked in the shipped build.
- A model that reproduces a compared implementation's pixels reads every stage a pixel passes through, from the data reduction to the fragment shader.
- When the version of a compared library changes, the claims that cite the old version are checked again in the new one.
- A design that ports another implementation's algorithm names the version it ports and reads that implementation's change log (NEWS, release notes) for changes to the values up to that version. R's `density()` changed its kernel grid in 4.4.0 (`old.coords`, PR#18337, densities by a factor of about 0.999); PR #223 read the trunk source without naming the version, a review recalled the old grid, and the NEWS settled it. The tests' reference is made with a version on the same side of such a change.
- A design that picks the side a compared implementation avoids for a stated reason writes what that reason costs, and its acceptance measures it.

## What a design writes down

- A decision that changes one case of an algorithm is written together with the algorithm's general rule, so that the case does not read as a new rule.
- An option a Grill offers relies only on what the code or the design already has, checked before the Grill; an option that needs something new says so and offers adding it. PR #112's Q3 gave the theme a panel color while `RenderList` drew only polylines and text; the fill command was found when the design was written and had to be asked again (Q15). PR #169's Q2 offered a color per element for rules, segments, and error bars; a polyline record holds one `LineStyle` and is drawn three times, so 131,072 elements would have been 131,072 polylines, found while writing and asked again. A choice that changes what a record carries reads the record type and how the backend draws it first.
- Every type in a signature the design writes is defined in the design or the code.
- A design that fixes the shape of a public C# API compiles its signatures and its sample code in a scratch project with the repository's settings (implicit usings, C# 14, `InvariantGlobalization`, the analyzers) before the design PR writes them. The diagnostics that turn up there and not on paper: a call with `null` or with no argument that two overloads make ambiguous (CS0121: `Dash(LineDash?)` beside `Dash(params ReadOnlySpan<float>)` in PR #129), a name that collides with an implicit using (CS0104), a type hidden by a namespace of the same name inside `HyperPlot.*` (CS0118), a public member that shows an internal type (CS0053), a `ref` field to a `ref struct` (CS9050), an interpolated-string handler that cannot take a receiver by `ref` (CS1620), and `CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture`, which `InvariantGlobalization` makes invariant. A positional member of a nullable type named as its type (`Color? Color` in a record struct) does not get the rule that lets a name mean both the member and the type: inside the type, `Color` is the property, and a static member of the type is written `HyperPlot.Color.…`. PR #154 wrote four public types without this compile, and the review asked for it.
  - The sample that the PR body says was compiled is cut from the design file as it stands in the pushed head, not written again by hand. PR #77's body named a §4.9 example that the file did not hold.
  - A claim about what the language allows (who can derive from a type, what a copy constructor may be) is compiled in the same scratch. PR #77 said users could not derive from the spec records; a record that is not sealed must have a `protected` copy constructor (CS8878), and users can derive through it.
  - A test value the design writes for an edge case is evaluated in the scratch to reach the branch it is for, before it goes in, a value taken from a review included. PR #77 took `1e15 + 1e-3` from a review for a narrow domain; it equals `1e15`, and the test would have stopped at another check.
- A fact that the code holds today (which golden scenes a new default changes, which callers use a member) is read and written by the design PR, not sent to the implementation PR to "read and decide". PR #154 sent which scenes a new default color redraws; read, none changed, and the implementation PR can now tell a baseline that moves when it should not. The facts of the scenes at hand do not stand for the general case: PR #177 found that only golden scene 13 changed, true because every scene has a title or a legend, and left out that a plot without them moves its edge labels every time; the design writes such consequences, and a test of them.
- A golden scene the design defines is drawn, in the design PR, by the design's own rules. PR #185 put value labels on bars whose domain left no room above the tallest; the same PR learns the domain without the labels' boxes, so that label fell outside the panel and was clipped. So are the scene's counts (cells, panels, empty places): PR #255 put four cells' worth of plots on a 2 × 3 grid and called one cell empty; the review counted two.
- A design that adds public types says whether they belong to the public surface of design §4.9 and `.cursor/rules/csharp.mdc`, and changes both when they do; the shape of a type does not settle whether it is public.
- A stat or an aggregation computed in a scale's space says, for each pass (counts, moments, quantile ranks, binning), what happens to values the transform does not map to a finite number (0 and below on a log scale). PR #223 left it out, and the review found it.
- A derived structure whose size its parameters bound (a derived table, a grid) writes its largest size and the memory of it. PR #223's moving average can reach 131,072 points × 256 groups, about 540 MB, and the review asked for the formula.
- A design that adds a scale or another element of a view says how the view's operations (§6.2: zoom, pan, history, links) act on it, or names the row that decides it. PR #231 added secondary scales without saying what a zoom does to them; the review found it, and the owner sent it to row I1.
- A design that adds a second scale to a direction says, for each stage that reads a scale (learning, ticks, mapping, M4's boundaries, the diff), which of the two it reads. PR #231 said it for learning and ticks only; the review asked for the mapping and the boundaries.
- A spec node that holds a reference to a user's interface (`IScale`, `IStat`) says how the diff compares it (the reference, the values beside it) and that a host reuses the instance, as row G2's `TransformScaleSpec` does. PR #231's `SecondaryAxisSpec` left it out, and a host that made a new `IScale` each frame would lay out every frame.
- The design's golden scenes are checked against the combinations the same design refuses. PR #231's scene (34) put a secondary axis on a time x, which the PR refused; it now uses seconds.
- A design that adds a channel reading a column says how a change of that column's values reaches the plan: the version the plan reads for it. PR #173 first left the column of element colors out of the layer's columns, and an `Invalidate` of it alone drew the old colors (the review's finding).
- A type that uses only a contract (an interface of `HyperPlot.Rendering`, the BCL) lives beside the contract, not in one implementation of it. PR #35 first put `HeadlessRenderer` in the D3D11 project, so that each later backend would write it again.
- A design that names the type or method a call goes in reads which project that type is in and what the project may reference (design §2.2): Core references no Rendering or host project.
- A new public command or API states what it does with an argument that is NaN, infinite, empty, or out of range, as the APIs before it do. Data with NaN is a normal input for a plotting library, not an error.
- A sentence that a design PR writes or keeps about how existing code works (a search, a correction loop, an order) is read against that code first. PR #86 kept "the guess is checked once on each side" in §5.5.1; the code already walked to the right index before row D2, and PR #89's review found it. So is what an earlier row's API anchors at: PR #112 placed tick labels "at the tick with `Center`", while row T1's horizontal alignment aligns lines inside the block and the block's left edge is always the position; PR #116 found it in SpecPlot's image and places a label by its measured width. A list of the code a change reaches (the columns that read whole-column statistics) is made by searching the call, not from the cases remembered: PR #244 listed the rect, segment, text, and rule columns from memory, and `rg TryGetRange` found a bar's x and a stat's x too.
- A design that reuses an earlier row's path or step ("as row X does", "the same as one panel") lists the inputs that path or step assumes and says what each one is in the new case. PR #239 first sent facets down row G7c's split path, which splits by one column, while a grid's panels are a pair of columns; took the color bar's length from the panel's height, while facets have two heights (one panel and the grid); and limited edge labels by the figure's rectangle, while facet panels meet across a gap. The review found all three. The path is read in the code before the design PR, not only in the design: PR #239 also took row G7c's run path for panels, where only the plain geoms' plans take a row range and a run learns from the whole column's statistics; the implementation found it, and PR #244 had to correct the design.
- A sentence that replaces another keeps what the old one said and still holds. PR #77 replaced "`Dispose` releases its own target; the owner releases `backend`" with a sentence about `Render(PlotSpec)`, and the lifetime of `Dispose` and `backend` left the design.
- A design that reads a summary of the data (chunk statistics, a pyramid) for a rule reads which values the summary leaves out. `ChunkStats<T>` leaves out NaN only, so a domain learned from it can have an infinite end; PR #77 first wrote a rule for "no finite value" that the statistics cannot tell.
- A check the design adds on an existing type is read against the code that makes the type (its constructors and factories). A check that cannot fail is not written: PR #77 checked that a `UniformColumn`'s step is positive, which `Column.Uniform` already refuses to break. PR #239 refused a facet column of another length, which `DataTableBuilder.Build` already refuses to build; PR #211's review had taught it.
- A design that waits for background work names the member it waits on, and says what the wait does after the work is invalidated and started again, and when the work fails. PR #77 first said "wait for the pyramid", while `Completion` reports only the first build.
- A design that changes the unit of a quantity (data units, plot-area pixels, physical pixels, WPF device-independent pixels) lists every API that carries it across a boundary, arguments and return values, input coordinates and hit tests included, and writes the unit of each.
- A quantity divided by a width or a distance (a density, a rate) names the space the width is measured in, for each scale kind (linear, log, time). PR #215 wrote a density's width "in data units", which on a log or a time x is not ggplot2's value; the owner chose the scale's space.
- A public interface the library calls (a user's stat, geom, or scale) states how it is called: how often, on what lengths of input, from which thread, in what order, and what the implementation must satisfy (an associative merge, a pure function). PR #215's `IStat.Fold` said "a chunk's values of a cell", which reads as once a chunk; the review asked for the call contract.
- A design that changes a value an existing step reads (where a label's box lies, a band's width, a domain's ends) lists the formulas that read it and changes each. PR #177 moved the edge labels inward and first left the band axis's k as it was: k₀ and the jump past an overlapping pair did not count the shift, so a pair cut short at k could still be too close at the next k.
- A type in a design signature that holds a size or a coordinate is checked against the scale factors the design supports (125 %, 150 %) and against the range of the data (a float holds 24 bits: a position far from its origin loses whole pixels, §3.6).
- A design that computes a value approximately checks the approximation against every field of the output built from it (positions, order, roles), not only against the value, and checks a read it claims to save against what the other fields of the same output read. The D0 error-bounded min/max query could pick an element outside its pixel column and break M4's order, and the raw reads it saved sat on the pages first and last read anyway. An approximation that paints writes how far its pixels can be from the exact shape: PR #121 flattened an area's column that holds points and first left out that it paints up to the neighbouring segment's slope times one pixel beyond the line.
- A promise that a rewriting keeps results ("the same formula as a matrix, so no pixel moves") writes the order of the floating-point operations, not only the algebra. PR #125 first mapped through a matrix's elements, scale times translation multiplied out, and the exact tests of rows G1 and G2 moved pixels by 1e-14; the transform now keeps its factors and applies them in the formula's order.
- A row that adds a caller of a public interface (`IScale`, `IGeom`, a callback) reads the interface's documented contract (the unit of a call, how often, which thread) against the new calls. PR #125 called `IScale.Forward` one value at a time for a step's turns, bars, error bars, and rules, while its XML said it was never called per data point; the review found it.
- A Grill option that chooses how a drawing composes its pieces (the coverage of each piece combined by a maximum, by a sum, by a union of samples) is compared with the exact area in a CPU prototype before the round is sent, over shapes where pieces only touch and where they overlap. PR #129's first choice combined the distance-based coverage of segment boxes and join pieces by a maximum; where a box and a join piece only touch, both cover half a pixel, and a thick line showed a seam inside its bends (0.32 from the exact area at a 30° join). The prototype was run only while the design was written, and the Grill was asked again.
- An error a prototype measured is written with the scenes it measured, not as a bound. A bound comes from a scan of the inputs that make the worst case (every edge angle and offset for a fixed sample pattern): PR #129 wrote "within 0.08" from four scenes, and a straight edge at a slope near 1/5 reached 0.10.
- A bound on how many records a call makes counts every input that multiplies it: the runs a NaN breaks a polyline into, not one polyline. PR #129 wrote "segments + 2" for the pieces of joins and caps; a polyline of r runs makes up to S + r, and the 2-point runs of segment, rule, and error-bar layers make 2S.
- A formula the design writes with a rounding says what happens at exactly one half, best as an integer formula. PR #35 wrote `round(c · 255 / a)`, which is 42.5 for `k = 1, a = 6`: half up gives 43, `Math.Round` gives 42, and the test's expected value depended on the reading.
- A figure the design derives (a memory bound, a count, a level count of a pyramid) writes its arithmetic beside it. So does a limit on a count that a rule changes: PR #129 wrote that an odd dash pattern may have 4 intervals before repetition, while 4 is even and the largest odd count that stays within 8 once doubled is 3; the implementation's XML copied it.
- A sentence that states what a formula leads to (how large a texel is at the chosen level, how many tiles are visible at most) is checked with one example of numbers put in for each direction or case. PR #197 chose the level by ⌊log₂ max(sx, sy)⌋ and wrote the texels' sizes swapped with their counts and the other direction's range backwards; its worst tile count went 170, 216, and 160 through two reviews. sx = 3, sy = 1 would have shown both.
- A limit derived from a shared resource (the bytes sent per frame, a cache the plot owns) is counted over everything that shares the resource, and the design names where that resource lives: per layer, per series, or per plot. PR #185 capped text labels at 4,096 per layer from the frame's transfer and the plot's shaping cache; three layers would have overflowed the cache every frame.
- A new check of a value takes the form of the existing check of the same kind of value: the same exception, at the same stage. PR #185 left a text size above 512 after scaling to `DrawText` while drawing; the theme's text sizes are checked at the start of the compile.
- A rule that says a case is handled "like" an existing mechanism ("dropped like NaN") is checked against every stage that mechanism goes through, and writes the stages where they differ. PR #96 said a value that a scale maps to a non-finite number breaks the line like NaN; the pyramid leaves NaN out before M4 picks its points, while such a value is dropped after, so the pixel column's minimum and maximum are not kept, and the review found it.
- A rounding is written with its direction as ⌊⌋ or ⌈⌉, not as "truncate" or "round down", and a negative value is among its tests. PR #96 wrote "truncate the lower end" for a date-time domain; before the epoch the value is negative, and truncation toward 0 and the floor differ by one unit.
- When two rules apply to the same input (a learned domain of one value and `Zero`), the design writes their order, and a test takes a value whose result depends on it (v = 5 gives [0, 5] or [0, 5.5]).
- A rule that rejects candidates by a hard limit, used with a step that changes the limit's input (nice widening a domain), is checked on a case near the limit with both applied. PR #116's log ticks with nice widened every fitting skip past the count limit, and the next skip left fewer than two ticks inside the domain: no tick at all. Golden scene (7) showed it, and the owner chose to fall back to no nice.
- A design that sends values through a transform and its inverse (boundaries through `Inverse`, points through `Forward`) writes the error of the round trip and the promise it loosens, and names the path on which a precision promise (row D2's exact differences) is lost.
- A claim that a floating-point step is exact ("subtracting an integer does not round") is checked with exact arithmetic (fractions, `BigInteger`) over each range of sign and magnitude before it goes in, and is written with its bound where it is not exact. PR #86 wrote that re-anchoring does not round; it rounds by up to 2^−54 for a lower difference in (−0.5, 0), and an upper difference of 2^53 or more rounds too. A test of the step takes its tolerance from that bound.
- A design that adds an exact form of a value (an integer start beside a `double` one) checks that every public caller that needs the value can read it exactly. A property that returns the `double` rounds again what the row was meant to keep: PR #86 first gave integer uniform columns only `double` accessors, while callers that connect the public M4 themselves need the exact start to build their origin and boundaries.
- A design that puts a component "in Core" names the object that owns it and the way each user reaches that same object: another assembly, another public call. PR #105 put the shaping cache and the glyph atlas in Core without an owner, so whether measuring and drawing shared one cache was open until the review; the owner became `TextEngine`, one per backend.
- An eviction rule names its unit of time (a frame, a call) and what must not move while it is in use. PR #105 evicted glyphs "not used in the current frame", while `Draw` runs outside frames and many times in one, and packing a shelf again would have moved glyphs the current `Draw` had already placed.
- A design that builds positions from what an OS API reports covers every direction the API reports (a right-to-left run, the sign of an offset) and every input that changes the result (the locale of font fallback), read in the header's own words. PR #105 wrote left-to-right runs only and fixed the locale to "en-us".
- An acceptance that checks with a fake implementation names the hook where the fake is plugged in.
- A design that keeps a cache or a state per item (per series, per chunk, per tile, per pyramid level) writes its size as a formula of the content, and its acceptance bounds it with a number a test checks. Reporting the size is not a bound: PR #169 wrote nk × nx × 16 B for the extremes of a categorical bar split by color and left the cap as a question in the PR body, and the review set 2^24. A value that only some items use goes behind a reference of its own, made for the items that use it; fields added to a side object that every item holds are paid by every item.
- A design that allocates by a size the content decides (an offscreen layer as large as its content, a density texture as large as the plot) writes what cuts the size (the clip, the target), the cap (D3D11 textures are at most 16,384 pixels a side), and what is drawn when the cap refuses it.
- A drawing primitive's definition is checked against every shape the geoms built on it give it, the degenerate ones included (an edge that crosses the other). PR #121 defined a column's coverage as 0 where its top edge fell below its bottom edge, and an area whose line crossed its baseline left the rest of that column unpainted; the coverage is now the area between the two edges, in either order.
- A default given in data units is checked through every kind of scale. PR #121's baseline of 0 maps to −∞ on a log axis, so areas and bars drew nothing until it was put at the panel's edge.
- A limit derived from a requirement keeps the requirement's unit (an amount, a pixel count), and is checked against earlier decisions about the same input. PR #121 turned R-1's "a few MB a frame" into W × H / 16 elements, which would have thrown when a window shrank, against row G3's rule that a small target draws only the background; the limit is now the constant 131,072.
- A pass that reads or clears what another pass wrote covers at least what the first pass wrote. PR #43 first had the color and reset passes of a polyline cover the points' bounding box, while the coverage pass wrote half the width and 1 pixel beyond it, so the anti-aliased band got no color and its coverage stayed for the next polyline.
- A design that bounds what a writer allocates checks the output format first: a format that puts a length before its data (a PNG chunk, a length-prefixed record) needs the whole of it in memory or a seekable stream. PR #35 wrote one `IDAT` and an allocation of one row, which cannot both hold; the PNG now goes out in `IDAT` chunks of a fixed buffer.
- A design that adds a new kind of edge (a clip, a plot-area border) says whether it is put on pixels by the rounding the other edges take, and under the same condition; otherwise a gap or a seam shows where the two edges meet.
- A rule that puts a line on pixels is checked at an odd and an even rounded width against the area beside the line. PR #112 centered the axis line on the panel's edge, and at an even width its inner half covered the panel's last row of data; the center now sits half the width outside the edge.
- A mechanism applied per command (a clip, a style, an opacity) says which kinds of command and mark it applies to, text included.
- A type that takes a visual value (a line width, a point size, how a color scale interpolates, a blur radius) says what each value means, after listing how each compared implementation reads it, with lines.
- A design that reads data from a file or a stream treats it as untrusted input and writes its limits (size, how much it allocates, the structure it accepts) and what happens past them. Those limits bound what is allocated, not only what is read: a size the input claims (a row count, a chunk length) is checked against what the input can hold (the file's length) before a buffer of that size is made or a range is mapped, and a test measures the allocation of the worst input.
- A limit the design sets on a count (64 color maps for a backend, slots in a page) writes what happens past it: an exception that names the item and the limit, an eviction, or the path of degradation. The code never substitutes another entry: PR #201's 65th color map took row 0, another map's colors, through `Math.Max(0, …)`, because §5.2 wrote the limit alone; the review made it an `InvalidOperationException`.
- A design that puts outside data into the repository (reference data sets, test images, fonts) names its source, its version, and its license, quoted from the file in the distribution.
- A design that adds threads, workers, or OS resources writes their lifetime: who creates them, when they stop, who releases them, and what happens when they cannot be created.
- A public type the design gives an OS resource or an `IDisposable` field becomes a type to dispose (CA1001); the design says so, or picks a form that needs none and says which. PR #55 found it only in the build: the design's `ManualResetEventSlim` would have made `M4Batch` disposable, and the wait became a monitor.
- A design in which several threads can touch the same item (a chunk a worker builds while a reader reads it and another worker could take it) writes the item's states as a table, the transitions it allows, which transition a writer must win before it writes, and who queues the item again after a change in the middle of the work. The C2 design first had none, so two writers and a pyramid that completed on an old generation were both possible.
- A design that calls an OS or GPU API from several threads where its documentation says nothing about threads checks correctness apart from speed: the parallel result is the serial one, byte for byte, over hundreds of repetitions.
- A design split into steps (a coarse level now, finer levels later; a worker that rebuilds while the frame reads) says which arrays a later step rewrites, which version their readers see, and how a reader that holds an old version is kept from reading freed memory.
- A design that removes a dependency lists every place that calls it now and what replaces each.
- A contract that hands work to an OS or GPU API is written after reading that API's output parameters, not only its inputs: what it returns when it finds nothing, and what else it returns.
- A design that reads a GPU or OS result some frames later says, for each host path, which call sends the work: a window's `Present` does, a headless frame has none. PR #59 first read timestamps with `D3D11_ASYNC_GETDATA_DONOTFLUSH` alone, which on the headless path can wait forever for commands that were never sent.
- A design that adds the shape of a public API checks it against the API rules of `.cursor/rules/csharp.mdc` one by one before the PR (no throwing because of a call's stage, states as types). PR #59 first made an unpaired `EndFrame` throw, and the review found the rule; `BeginFrame` now returns a `FrameScope`.
- A design that adds nested scopes to a mechanism with states checks every order of opening, closing, and state change. PR #59 missed a frame that saw a device loss and was closed after an inner call had made the device again: the close would have ended a query that never began.
- A design that adds a state to keep a property (a flag, a mark, a counter) first traces whether a release or a reset that already exists gives the property. PR #59 gave a frame that saw a loss a mark; the loss already released the queries, and the timer made again had no frame open, so the mark changed nothing. Two mutants survived in PR #63 and the mark went.
- A design that says an OS API does something names the version that has that capability (the interface number and the overload), after reading its declaration in the SDK the build uses.
- A design that uses a value read from the OS or the host (a DPI, a refresh rate, an adapter) says which unit it is read for (the system, the window's monitor, the visual), when it is read, and when it is read again. Values read from a host's message or event name the field they come from, or the call that reads them when the message has none, after reading its documentation.
- A design that draws with an OS component or a GPU feature it has not used measures the first-use cost and the memory in a scratch prototype before the Grill decides, at the scales the design supports (100, 125, and 150 %).
- A design that changes what an algorithm reads checks every cache and boundary rule against the change, the path that hits a cache included.
- A design that adds a path to a component that exists reads how the component registers and takes its work, and checks the invariants against that. PR #51 first had `PlotWorkerPool.Extract` queue a batch as the pool queues pyramids, by making a new array each time, which allocates on every frame against invariant 3.
- A step that hands one call's work to several workers checks the arguments they share once, before it hands them out. PR #51 first left the boundaries to each series, so a caller's mistake would have shown as every series failing.
- A design of what drives a frame or a loop lists every call that the code driving it today makes (a sample, a benchmark scene) and checks each against its own steps.
- A design that calls a user's handler in the middle of changing its own state says what happens when the handler comes back into it: a frame run synchronously, a message pumped inside the handler, the spec or the data changed inside the handler.
- A mechanism where a stage clears marks only for the items of a list says that every path that sets the mark puts the item in the list.
- A design in which pending work asks for the next turn of a loop (a frame, a worker pass) says which work asks for it, including work a turn puts back after a failure, so that a step that keeps failing does not run every frame.
- A value a later stage needs that an earlier stage resolves by rules that differ case by case is kept from that stage's result and read, not resolved again.
- An acceptance that a change allocates nothing is checked against each API that makes the change.
- A design that stops a propagation with a comparison decides two things in the design itself: everything the propagation carried, and what happens when a value to compare is gone.

## Judge the current optimum

The design records a decision made at that time. It is not proof that the decision is still the best one.

- Recommend the best solution for the problem as it stands now.
- Do not cite an existing sentence as the reason to keep that shape.
- Do not add public API only so an older shape can stay.
- If the written decision is no longer the best one, say so and change the design through the process above.

```text
# BAD — the design says "as datashader does" for the density path, and its PR body says datashader was not read
# GOOD — the design names datashader's file and lines for each step it shares, and the steps it does not share are HyperPlot's own
```
